The loader's internals. `impl::object` is one mapped shared object: its path, a copy of its
program headers, its `elf::image`, its pages, its reference count, the objects it needs and
its lookup scope. `impl::load`, `impl::release` and `impl::lookup` are what `dl::library`
calls.

The objects live in one registry (an array of `ca::unique<object>` on `ca::heap`, so an object
never moves) behind one mutex. The running program's `elf::image` is read under that mutex on
the first load and kept (the program never moves; no global constructor), and every load's
session points at it for symbol resolution. A load works in phases on the objects it maps, in the order it
mapped them, breadth first from the root: map, resolve DT_NEEDED, build each scope, apply the
relative relocations of all, then the symbolic ones from the last mapped to the first (so a
dependency's indirect functions are callable when its users bind to them), protect RELRO, and
run the initializers depth first. Any failure releases the root, which unmaps every object the
load made and gives back every reference it took.

`impl::tls` is the dynamic thread vector. A fixed table of `tls_modules` entries, each the
image, sizes and alignment of one PT_TLS segment, and a generation that changes at every
registration and release, published last; each thread has a `thread_local` array of slots
indexed by module id, each a block and the generation it was made for. `tls_get_addr`, the
loader's `__tls_get_addr`, compares the two and makes a fresh block on a mismatch, so a module
id handed to another object never shows the old one's data. It takes no lock: the table only
changes under the loader's lock, and code reaching a module's variables runs while the module is
loaded. The first registration creates a thread key whose exit callback frees the thread's
blocks. `walk_objects` lets the symbolizer visit what is loaded
(`ca::trace::impl::loaded_objects`), set on the first load so a program that never loads
anything does not link the loader through the symbolizer.
